t along  with the guide page on the Wolfram  language gallery on how to make a 3d  image from an elevation map and having  flown over Mount st. Helens many times  in my life as my family travels from San  Diego to Spokane I wanted to try to  print it so just using the guide page  and entities here I pulled up the county  that includes Mount Saint Helens  took that section this was in the guide  page so I did it too and then I made a  list plot 3d and then applied the plot  theme film surface so the next thing I  knew is I had Mount st. Helens ready to  print and sure enough I did and I was  able to you know we were able to do this  in fact I'm just sharing this little bit  of code with another student for a final  project she's depict another mountain  and was looking at ingredients and all  sorts of things and had was able to  reproduce her own 3d print similar one  thing about printing with the styles  that the resolution is only as good as  the original image I had some I had to  estimate what I thought the appropriate  height would be because the blacks and  the whites were going from just zero to  one  and all of the pixels were the x and  y-axes  so it was like a pixel number so it  wasn't in like kilometers or some thing  that was relevant to the image and then  I was messing around with a picture of  my kids and I was hoping this would  still be getting passed around because  what happened here is I didn't even  bother to print this one because I knew  it just looked weird I'm like these are  my kids and it looks so strange and then  on Friday we were saying yeah the  printers open why don't I try to print  it and it looked kind of weird as we  expected but then I whatever reason held  it up to the light and all of a sudden I  had a photo of my kids in 3d print so  that was a really a treat so make sure  you get a bright light behind that one  to see it so yet another approach to  creating 3d models I wanted to create  something for our with our school mascot  logo here the Torrey Pines Falcons go  Falcons class of 92 and I got this from  one of our staff members as a PDF file  and figured out that if I did boundary  dust critize graphics I could get a  region from that because I had noticed  something I had never done before that  in plot 3d I can tell it to plot all X&amp;Y  point plot the function over all  coordinates that are within the region  so just by creating that region and I  added a quadratic function to it I could  create this Falcon shape that kind of  lifts off the page then applying the  same technique I created my own PDF file  of the Falcons text but now instead of  applying a quadratic function I just  wanted it all to be the same height and  then putting them together here gave me  this and I added a cylindrical base and  created this final product and there's  actually a handful of them floating  around our school right now and it's  kind of fun to see that parametric plot  3d also what I when I did the Falcon  that was with filled surface as well  kind of like I did with the Mount st.  Helens parametric plot 3d I used the  plot theme thick surface in order to  have a surface to print I've also done  that with plot 3d as  well but I would I would like to be able  to control the thickness level so if any  of you out here in the audience have the  power to make that happen this is my  plea to you unless there's an option I  just haven't found in the documentation  center because you know that happens too  so I'd like to be able to have it be you  know maybe a little bit thicker or a  little bit narrower I'm sure with  additional work I could do that and I  created this cup this is kind of a low  resolution version of it because the 400  plot points was just going to make the  file size here too big but again I'm  using plot points to adjust the  resolution but that goes back to the  whole computational time and file sizes  and like I'm letting things render  before I go to bed and then hoping that  