nd  and took measurements and then they  printed more and more and more blades  because they wanted each one to have a  slightly different adjustment and then  they recorded their data and did  analysis and it was really fun to see  what they came up with and I had only  started working with 3d printing in  March and they were these are my  students now presenting this in May so  it didn't take much for the students to  get excited and get involved another  student will he was interested in the  brick ista chrome problem and looking  the you know seeing how a marble would  fall on a cycloid shape versus a  parabola or a straight line so he  followed basically the same technique  that I did to make the falcon head in  terms of using discretize graphics and  plotting 3d plot over the regions and he  made a cycloid and then turned it into  three dimensions and did all some  transformed regions and then we did this  he did this for the parabola and the  line and then kind of added some  additional graphics to make this ramp  and it was actually quite large it was  the largest thing we had printed at the  time and immediately I brought it to  class and I brought a few marbles and  the kids sat down and someone pulled out  their phone and did a slow-mo video so  we could see what would happen and  testing out the ramps and the result so  it's was quite powerful to be able to  take this idea that they had talked  about in physics and so get the right  shot here so you can see that it  actually wins  to take this idea that they'd studied  about in physics and then use  Mathematica to create something and then  we were able to print it and then use it  as an actual like physical demonstration  in class so that's what was exciting  examples of things you could do for  other classes a a teacher came to the  lunch room one day oh she was with all  these little pieces of paper that she  had cut out because she was doing these  different area problems with her  geometry class and